WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.320
What does a thread share

2
00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.120
with its process? A thread

3
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.860
is an execution path with

4
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.080
its own program counter, registers

5
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.760
and stack. Threads inside one

6
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.520
process share that process address

7
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.900
space and resources, so two

8
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.780
stacks do not mean two

9
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:15.840
isolated worlds.

10
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.280
Picture a server with two

11
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.020
threads, T one and T

12
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.320
two. Both can execute the

13
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.480
same program code and access

14
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.780
shared process data. Open files

15
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.720
belong among the process resources

16
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.440
they can share rather than

17
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.060
being copied into a completely

18
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.000
private file system for each

19
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.260
thread.

20
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.360
Each thread still needs a

21
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.540
private current instruction position and

22
00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.500
saved registers. Its stack holds

23
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.980
its own active calls and

24
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.040
local execution state. If one

25
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.760
thread pauses inside a function,

26
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.940
resuming another thread must restore

27
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.100
that other thread context, including

28
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.220
the correct stack.

29
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.780
Watch T one call a

30
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.240
function and push a frame

31
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.240
onto its stack. T two

32
00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.540
can call the same function

33
00:00:58.700 --> 00:00:59.680
but use a different frame

34
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:00.780
on its own stack. Their

35
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.500
code is shared; their active

36
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:03.820
call frames are distinct.

37
00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.340
Now both update a shared

38
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.900
counter initially five. If they

39
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.500
both read five and each

40
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.120
later writes six, one increment

41
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.720
is lost. Their private registers

42
00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.520
and stacks did not protect

43
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.340
that shared data; a synchronization

44
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.860
mechanism must coordinate the conflicting

45
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:23.780
accesses.

46
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.380
Threads can improve responsiveness when

47
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.300
one execution path waits and

48
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:29.920
another remains able to run.

49
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.660
With appropriate kernel execution contexts,

50
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.660
one thread can block on

51
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.620
input-output while another uses a

52
00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.360
processor. The mapping matters: a

53
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.020
blocking kernel call can stall

54
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.940
all user threads sharing only

55
00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:43.960
one kernel thread.

56
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.440
On one processor core, ready

57
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.340
threads alternate; they are not

58
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.880
executing instructions simultaneously on that

59
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.160
same core. With multiple cores

60
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.780
and enough independently schedulable kernel

61
00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.240
contexts, threads can actually run

62
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.960
in parallel. Creating extra logical

63
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.520
threads alone does not add

64
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.360
hardware cores.

65
00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.300
Compared with separate processes, sharing

66
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.540
an address space can make

67
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:10.080
cooperation convenient and reduce some

68
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.120
management costs. It also means

69
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.240
a mistaken shared-memory access can

70
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.360
affect peer threads. The benefit

71
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.500
is not a guarantee that

72
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.460
every threaded program becomes faster.

73
00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.100
For the examination, classify code,

74
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.240
shared data and open resources

75
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.480
on the process side. Classify

76
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.920
thread identifier, program counter, registers

77
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.260
and stack on the thread

78
00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.080
side. If the question asks

79
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.540
about an actual implementation, use

80
00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.100
its stated mapping rather than

81
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.780
silently assuming one thread per

82
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:38.080
core.

83
00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:39.700
Things to take note of.

84
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.160
Separate shared resources from private

85
00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.200
execution state, concurrency from parallelism,

86
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.740
and possible responsiveness from guaranteed

87
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:49.660
speedup. When a trace contains

88
00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.840
a shared variable, ask who

89
00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.160
can reach the same storage

90
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.100
before claiming that private stacks

91
00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:55.720
make it safe.
